# What must not reach a container image.
#
# ## Why this file exists
#
# `Dockerfile.manager` says tests, policy and docs "have no business in a running image", and copies
# `src/` wholesale — so 14 test files (232 KB of 600 KB) shipped anyway. The comment described an
# intention that nothing enforced. Measured by listing the built image.
#
# The size is not the point. `policy/` is the one that matters: it is a map of the site's addresses and
# open ports, and this image is pushed to a registry. It was not reaching the image only because the
# Dockerfile copies `src/` and `bin/heliopause-manager.ts` by name — a single future `COPY . .` would
# have put the whole site into a registry artifact, and nothing would have said so.
#
# So the exclusions are stated here as well as implied by the copy list. Defence in depth for the one
# case where the failure is silent and irreversible: once pushed, a layer has been distributed.

# ── Site data — must never be in an image ─────────────────────────────────────
policy/
docs/
pki*/
artifacts/
security-audits/
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/*.pkcs12
**/*.jks
**/*.keystore
**/*.kdb
**/*.kdbx
**/*.mobileprovision
**/*.der
**/*.pk8
**/*.p8
**/*.key
**/*.pem
**/*.csr
**/*.srl

# ## Patterns need `**/`
#
# `.dockerignore` matches against the whole path from the context root with Go's `filepath.Match`,
# which does not cross directory separators. So `*.test.ts` excludes only test files sitting at the
# root — `src/nft.test.ts` sails straight through. Written that way first and measured: the rebuilt
# image still carried all 14 test files, identical byte count, no error anywhere.

# ── Tests — not part of the runtime ───────────────────────────────────────────
# The manager runs `bin/heliopause-manager.ts` and the library it imports. A test file in a running
# image is dead weight that still gets scanned by every vulnerability tool pointed at the registry.
**/*.test.ts
src/test-util.ts

# ── Build and tooling context ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
.git/
.github/
node_modules/
scripts/
packaging/
agent/
**/*.md
tsconfig.json
# package-lock.json stays in the context. The web stage runs `npm ci` and that
# command reads the lockfile; excluding it was right when the image had no npm
# install. The first live C-pipeline of this Dockerfile failed on
# `COPY package.json package-lock.json ./` with "/package-lock.json": not found.
# The lockfile is not a secret, and the runtime stage still does not copy it.
